C/ Device-attribute inference (USA003087 Sec. 12.5): a channel is UNPAGED if
C/ any READ/READALL statement APPEARS for it in the compilation (paged only if
C/ used exclusively for WRITE). The rule is static -- which statements appear,
C/ not which execute -- so the READ(6) below, guarded off and never run, still
C/ makes channel 6 UNPAGED. On an UNPAGED device a CHARACTER WRITE is enclosed
C/ in apostrophes (USA003087 App. F), so 'HELLO' prints quoted; without the
C/ READ statement channel 6 would be PAGED and print HELLO unquoted. No input
C/ is consumed (the READ never executes). See run_all.sh.
 TPI: PROGRAM;
    DECLARE C CHARACTER(10);
    DECLARE B BOOLEAN INITIAL(FALSE);
    IF B THEN READ(6) C;
    WRITE(6) 'HELLO';
 CLOSE TPI;
